WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.784
- He's an entrepreneur,
motivational speaker,

00:00:02.784 --> 00:00:05.570
television personality, and author.

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:08.914
He turned down a career in
accounting to focus on sales,

00:00:08.914 --> 00:00:11.910
and now teaches others the art of selling.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.946
He's the author of five books
and has his the New York Times

00:00:14.946 --> 00:00:17.494
and Wall Street Journal Bestseller lists.

00:00:17.494 --> 00:00:19.234
He's Grant Cardone, and here are his

00:00:19.234 --> 00:00:21.094
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:21.094 --> 00:00:24.041
(loud swoosh)

00:00:24.041 --> 00:00:25.835
- Hey, I want to talk to
you today about success.

00:00:25.835 --> 00:00:29.244
Success, duty, obligation, responsibility,

00:00:29.244 --> 00:00:31.202
if you approach it any other way,

00:00:31.202 --> 00:00:33.322
which most people are, most people think,

00:00:33.322 --> 00:00:35.809
"Well, I'll be successful
if the economy works.

00:00:35.809 --> 00:00:38.473
"I'll be successful if something happens.

00:00:38.473 --> 00:00:40.739
"I'll be successful if I
win the lottery ticket."

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:43.973
Look, you're not going to be
successful if you don't demand it.

00:00:43.973 --> 00:00:46.171
If you literally don't
stick a claim to it and say,

00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:48.754
"This is mine. It's important,"

00:00:49.607 --> 00:00:51.101
you're not going to have it, I promise ya.

00:00:51.101 --> 00:00:53.110
I know. I'm telling you from experience.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:56.197
I spent the first 25 years of my life,

00:00:56.197 --> 00:00:59.822
like a spectator watching
other people get successful,

00:00:59.822 --> 00:01:01.405
and then wondering why I wasn't.

00:01:01.405 --> 00:01:03.972
Success is your duty.

00:01:03.972 --> 00:01:07.395
It is your obligation
and your responsibility.

00:01:07.395 --> 00:01:10.500
Almost 90% of sales managers
say that they don't have time,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.211
you know, and again, it goes back to,

00:01:12.211 --> 00:01:14.381
look, if you knew it was
going to bank you money today,

00:01:14.381 --> 00:01:17.232
would you make time
for it? So, they would.

00:01:17.232 --> 00:01:18.595
They would make time for
it 'cause they make time

00:01:18.595 --> 00:01:20.389
for the things that make 'em money.

00:01:20.389 --> 00:01:24.002
If you have a customer,
hopefully, if you have a customer,

00:01:24.002 --> 00:01:26.504
and you're working a
deal with that customer,

00:01:26.504 --> 00:01:28.774
and you got a hamburger in front of ya,

00:01:28.774 --> 00:01:30.467
hopefully you're going to
push the hamburger aside

00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:34.621
and work that deal because
the priority is to make money.

00:01:34.621 --> 00:01:36.998
In the same conversation,
that sales manager

00:01:36.998 --> 00:01:38.831
does not believe that,

00:01:40.148 --> 00:01:43.303
sales training converts
to, or allow our money,

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:44.707
so he doesn't make the time for it.

00:01:44.707 --> 00:01:46.610
When you say you don't have time,

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.587
the truth is you're saying,
"I'm not making it a priority

00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:51.331
"'cause I don't think it makes me money."

00:01:51.331 --> 00:01:53.998
So, you want to get
your work ethic so high

00:01:53.998 --> 00:01:55.645
that people actually admire you.

00:01:55.645 --> 00:01:57.197
I'm going to ask you a question right now.

00:01:57.197 --> 00:02:01.967
Do people know you because of
your unbelievable work ethic?

00:02:01.967 --> 00:02:03.712
If you can't answer yes to that man,

00:02:03.712 --> 00:02:06.326
you're not going to get in that top 10%.

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:07.666
You got to get to the top.

00:02:07.666 --> 00:02:10.031
People have to know you, not because,

00:02:10.031 --> 00:02:12.567
"Oh, he's a good salesman.
Oh, he's a good this.

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:14.744
"Oh, he's an artist.
Oh, he's a good pianist.

00:02:14.744 --> 00:02:18.318
"Oh, he writes a great, you know, story."

00:02:18.318 --> 00:02:21.843
No dude, people need to know
you for one major thing first.

00:02:21.843 --> 00:02:25.609
He works. He produces.
The guy's there everyday.

00:02:25.609 --> 00:02:27.776
The guy's pushing and
shoving because the truth is,

00:02:27.776 --> 00:02:30.486
no matter how good your ideas
are or how good your art is

00:02:30.486 --> 00:02:34.100
or how good your skillset is,
if you're not working, man,

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.523
if you're not vibrating at
a frequency that people say,

00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:38.403
"My God, how does that guy do all that,"

00:02:38.403 --> 00:02:41.549
if you're not vibrating
at that rate, 10 X levels,

00:02:41.549 --> 00:02:44.831
massive action, tremendous
work ethic that's just

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:47.894
the muscle now, and it's just
a discipline in your life.

00:02:47.894 --> 00:02:50.003
It's a way; it's a normal way

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:52.014
that other people think is abnormal.

00:02:52.014 --> 00:02:55.245
Ryan Seacrest, Tom Cruise,
Oprah, Steven Spielburg,

00:02:55.245 --> 00:02:58.924
Donald Trump still pumping
and working as a billionaire,

00:02:58.924 --> 00:03:00.070
look, if you're not working at that level,

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:01.748
you're not going to make it;
get your work ethic in, man.

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:05.312
It's the American way.
Work ethic! Check it out.

00:03:05.312 --> 00:03:07.207
The biggest struggle, man,
was just getting around

00:03:07.207 --> 00:03:10.663
the people that would say I could do it.

00:03:10.663 --> 00:03:12.859
I mean, that's the biggest struggle, just,

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:14.860
you get around one guy who
says, "Yeah, you can do it,"

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.539
and then you talk to him
tomorrow, and he's like,

00:03:16.539 --> 00:03:19.496
"Naw dude, it's freaking,
he can't do this yet."

00:03:19.496 --> 00:03:22.532
You know, there's so few
people telling you to do it.

00:03:22.532 --> 00:03:25.292
You know what there is a
lot of? Save your money.

00:03:25.292 --> 00:03:27.245
Protect yourself. Conserve.

00:03:27.245 --> 00:03:29.167
Don't spend. Don't take risk.

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:32.951
Taking risk is the new
way to be risk-adverse.

00:03:32.951 --> 00:03:37.143
So, the biggest struggle
for me was my family, man.

00:03:37.143 --> 00:03:39.857
The people closest to me
was the biggest struggle.

00:03:39.857 --> 00:03:43.400
Every time I went out to do
another deal, everybody told me,

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.037
"You don't need to do that, man."

00:03:46.037 --> 00:03:48.780
The only person that was
encouraging me was my twin brother,

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.120
'cause I would see how he was doing it.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.288
Dude, he was making he's swings,

00:03:52.288 --> 00:03:55.018
and so, every time I'd
get around other people,

00:03:55.018 --> 00:03:56.514
so, I'll tell you a little trick I did,

00:03:56.514 --> 00:04:00.519
on weekends, I would always go
see homes I couldn't afford.

00:04:00.519 --> 00:04:02.359
That has always inspired me.

00:04:02.359 --> 00:04:05.353
I would go look. I would
go drive neighborhoods.

00:04:05.353 --> 00:04:07.482
Literally, I've done this my entire life

00:04:07.482 --> 00:04:09.698
until I finally got to the point

00:04:09.698 --> 00:04:11.693
where they wouldn't even
let me in the neighborhood.

00:04:11.693 --> 00:04:13.950
There's neighborhoods in Miami,
dude, you can't even go see.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:16.068
They won't even let you in the gates.

00:04:16.068 --> 00:04:18.117
Most of 'em you can get in if
